WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.710
Daniel: The radar puts body systems and compact summaries
on one screen, so it is easy to see what to open before a
visit.

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:12.950
Matilda: It is a navigation heuristic over recorded data,
not a clinical risk calculator.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:19.270
Daniel: Open the liver card. Its score of one hundred is
based on just one of three measured markers.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:27.490
Matilda: AST and GGT are not recorded, so FIB four is
unavailable. The card keeps the missing inputs beside the
score.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:33.340
Daniel: The PGS view brings models, percentiles, coverage,
and population context together.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:45.090
Matilda: This profile has not verified its EUR reference
choice. A percentile is a position in a comparison
population, not the probability of disease; incomplete
scores stay separate.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:53.080
Daniel: The longevity section shows an APOE e2 and e3
reading beside what studies have reported about it.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:01:03.340
Matilda: Those associations are modest and vary across
studies. Five checked positions do not mean five
favourable variants, and this cannot predict lifespan.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:11.510
Daniel: Lifestyle gathers three-month changes in activity,
body composition, and recovery, with dates and the Garmin
source.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:19.260
Matilda: Trends add context for a conversation, not a
diagnosis. The view also shows which recovery measure
needs attention.

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:29.770
Daniel: Keep the assistant you prefer: use the Claude
skill or plugin, or connect ChatGPT and other models
through supported tools or a collected context.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:44.500
Matilda: Scholion aligns dates, units, and sources before
the model explains a result, and makes gaps visible.
Medical context sent to a model goes to that provider; the
model does not get access to your disk.
